.TH CLOSEOUT-LINT 1 "2026-08-18" "realisateur" "bashified verb"
.SH NAME
closeout-lint \- the deterministic half of session closeout: what did today leave behind?
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B closeout-lint
.br
.B closeout-lint
.I name
...
.br
.B closeout-lint
--strict
.RI [ name ...]
.br
.B closeout-lint
--repo
.I path
.br
.B closeout-lint
--allow-blind
.SH DESCRIPTION
Scans registered projects for what a finishing session is apt to leave: an
uncommitted change to a live script, a branch that was never pushed, a claim
recorded nowhere durable.
.PP
Deterministic on purpose. It makes no AI calls and spends nothing, so it can
run on every closeout without a budget question, and its findings are
reproducible by anyone who runs it again.
.PP
A dirty tree at exit is a failed run, not a handoff: an uncommitted change to
a live script is indistinguishable from an abandoned one, and the next
autocommit may adopt it under a human's name.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --strict
Exit 1 if any FLAG was printed.
.TP
.BI --repo " path"
Audit ONE working tree. Cross-project sections are skipped and the age gate
is ignored.
.TP
.B --allow-blind
A domain that could not be read warns instead of gating.
.TP
.BI HOURS= n
In the environment: how far back to look.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Scanned; no --strict, or --strict and nothing found.
.TP
.B 1
--strict, and at least one FLAG was printed.
.TP
.B 6
--strict, and a domain existed but was NOT read, with no --allow-blind.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR claim-drift (1),
.BR silence-audit (1),
.BR discipline (1)
